What is HVDC Transmission?
HVDC (High Voltage Direct Current) transmission systems are specialized power transmission solutions that use direct current for bulk electricity transfer, contrasting with conventional AC (Alternating Current) systems. These systems enable efficient long-distance power transmission with significantly lower electrical losses compared to AC systems, particularly advantageous for submarine cables and cross-border interconnections. Key components include converters, thyristor valves, smoothing reactors, and DC lines.
HVDC technology is uniquely positioned to address the intermittency and long-distance challenges of renewable integration. The technology enables asynchronous connections between renewable generation hubs and demand centers while providing grid stabilization capabilities. Recent projects demonstrate HVDC's ability to integrate 5 GW+ offshore wind farms, with converter stations capable of black start functionality to support grid resilience.
